ossibility. So, probably not the same..\n\nOption D: "There is no pie in the sky. if you don\'t believe this, you will be deceived sooner or later." The first part is a statement of impossibility—no pie in the sky. The second part is a conditional: if you don\'t believe this, then you\'ll be deceived. The structure here is similar in that it starts with an impossibility and then uses a conditional. However, the conditional here is about belief leading to deception, which is a consequence rather than an impossibility. The original argument\'s conditional led to an impossible event, so this might not be as similar..\n\nWait, let me think again. The original argument\'s structure is: A is impossible. If A, then B (where B is impossible). So, it\'s using the conditional to reinforce the impossibility of A by linking it to another impossibility. So, in the original, both A and B are impossible, and the conditional connects them..\n\nLooking back at option B: "Language cannot create wealth. if language can create wealth, then the boastful person is the richest in the world." Here, the first part is that A (language creating wealth) is impossible. The second part is if A, then B (boastful person is richest). Now, is B impossible? Well, it\'s not explicitly stated, but the argument is suggesting that if A were possible, then B would follow, which is a logical consequence. So, the structure is similar: stating A is impossible, then using a conditional where A leads to B, which may or may not be impossible, but the point is to use the conditional to support the initial statement..\n\nOption D, on the other hand, is more about a consequence of disbelief rather than linking to another impossibility. So, the structure isn\'t as close. Therefore, the option is ']]  3%|2         | 16/600 [00:18<05:43,  1.70it/s]
