E ranking for Silver County, which is a composite score based on standardized tests from grades 1 to 12. Last year, it was the lowest on record since 1964. This year, the testing results for grades 1-6 are about the same as last year, and the high school (grades 9-12) results are lower. Then there\'s a bold statement saying that the middle school (grades 7-9) results will also be as low or lower than last year\'s. Therefore, Silver County will establish a new low in its CEHE ranking..\n\nThe question is about the roles of the two boldface portions in the argument. The options are A, B, C, D, each describing different roles..\n\nFirst, I need to identify what each boldface part is doing. The first boldface is a prediction: "Surely the middle school testing results... will also be as low as, or lower than, last year\'s results." The second boldface is the conclusion: "Therefore, Silver County will undoubtedly establish a new low in its CEHE ranking.".\n\nSo, the first boldface is a claim or a prediction that supports the main conclusion. The second is the main conclusion itself..\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nOption A says the first is an objection the argument rejects, and the second is a conclusion if the objection stands. That doesn\'t fit because the first isn\'t an objection; it\'s a supporting claim..\n\nOption B says the first is a claim supporting a position the argument opposes, and the second supports the main conclusion. That doesn\'t fit because the first is part of the argument\'s support, not opposing it..\n\nOption C says the first is a prediction supporting the main conclusion, and the second is a conclusion drawn to support the main conclusion. Wait, the second is the main conclusion, so this might not be right because the second isn\'t supporting the main conclusion; it is the main conclusion..\n\nOption D says the first is a prediction supporting the main conclusion, and the second is the main conclusion. That fits because the first boldface is a prediction that, if true, supports the conclusion, and the second is the conclusion itself. Therefore, the option is ']] 10%|#         | 60/600 [01:13<09:03,  1.01s/it]
