actor here. Maybe it's a way to predict future health, but I'm not certain..\n\nOption B: Insuring only wealthy individuals. Well, wealthy people can pay for their own medical services, so they might not need to make claims as much. But wait, the policy is for elderly people, and if they're wealthy, they might not need the insurance because they can afford their own care. But the company wants low premiums to attract customers. If they only take wealthy people, maybe they can charge higher premiums, but the question says premiums must be low. So this might not be the best option because it could limit the number of customers they attract, especially if the premiums are low but the wealthy might not need the coverage as much..\n\nOption C: Insuring only those rejected by other companies. That sounds like they're taking on higher-risk individuals. If other companies rejected them, it's probably because they have higher chances of making claims. So insuring these people would likely lead to more claims and higher losses for Company X. That doesn't seem like a good strategy to minimize losses..\n\nOption D: Attracting middle-aged customers who won't submit claims for many years. This makes sense because if they're middle-aged, they're less likely to need the services right away. The policy is for elderly diseases, so middle-aged people would be covered but not using the benefits for a long time. This would give the company more time to collect premiums without having to pay out claims immediately. Over time, the company can build up a reserve, and when the customers do start making claims, they've already collected enough premiums to cover the costs. This seems like a solid strategy to minimize losses because it spreads out the risk over a longer period and reduces the immediate financial burden..\n\nWait, let me think again. The company is worried that the income from policies won't cover the claims. So they need a way to either reduce the number of claims or spread out the claims over time. Option D does both by targeting people who won't make claims for a long time, allowing the company to collect premiums without paying out much initially. That should help balance the income and expenses better. Therefore, the option is "]] 17%|#6        | 101/600 [02:36<34:16,  4.12s/it]
