g tea polyphenols is significantly prolonged..\n\nThis directly supports the context. Antioxidants inhibit free radicals, and longer lifespan in fruit flies shows that free radicals contribute to aging. So this is a supporting statement..\n\nOption D: Oxygen free radicals can lead to the release of human collagenase and hard elastin, resulting in excessive cross-linking and degradation of collagen and hard elastin in skin, resulting in skin loss of elasticity, cell aging and wrinkles..\n\nThis also supports the context. It explains how free radicals cause specific damage (to collagen and elastin), leading to visible signs of aging like wrinkles. So this is another supporting point..\n\nNow, going back to Option A. The context is about the harmful effects of oxygen free radicals causing aging. Option A is talking about a beneficial effect—enhancing the immune system. While it's true that free radicals can have both positive and negative roles in the body, the question is about which option cannot support the discussion. Since the discussion is focused on the negative impact leading to aging, Option A doesn't support that; instead, it provides a counterexample where free radicals are beneficial. Therefore, it cannot support the argument presented in the context. Therefore, the option is "]] 23%|##2       | 136/600 [03:12<10:59,  1.42s/it]
