Li wasn\'t there, so he can\'t be the criminal. That leaves no criminal, which contradicts statement 2. Therefore, at least one of the statements must be false..\n\nSo, looking at the options:.\n\nOption B says "At least one of the above statements is false." That seems correct because if all four were true, we have a contradiction. Therefore, at least one must be false..\n\nOption A says only one person committed the crime. Well, that\'s implied by statement 2, but the problem is that if all four statements are true, we end up with no criminal, which is impossible. So, A might not necessarily be correct because the statements could be contradictory, meaning the conclusion isn\'t valid..\n\nOption C says the evidence that Wang didn\'t commit the crime is false. That would mean Wang did it. But if statement 3 is false, then Wang is the criminal. But statement 4 says Li was elsewhere, so that\'s possible. However, we don\'t know for sure if statement 3 is the false one; it could be statement 4 that\'s false. So, we can\'t conclude that statement 3 is definitely false..\n\nOption D says Li Ming is definitely not in the auditorium. But statement 4 says he was there. If statement 4 is true, then D is false. But if statement 4 is false, then D is true. However, we don\'t know which statement is false, so we can\'t definitively say D is correct. Therefore, the option is ']] 26%|##6       | 156/600 [03:29<08:53,  1.20s/it]
