pulation by depleting their food supply. This directly weakens the original argument because it provides another plausible reason why seals could be responsible for the decline, even without predation..\n\nSo, comparing options C and D. Option C shows that the decline started before the increase in seals, which suggests that seals aren\'t the cause. Option D shows that seals and crocodiles compete for food, which means that even without eating crocodiles, seals could be causing their decline by reducing their food supply..\n\nThe question is about weakening the argument that seals aren\'t responsible because they don\'t eat crocodiles. So, if there\'s another way seals could be causing the decline, that weakens the argument. Therefore, option D is the stronger candidate because it introduces an alternative mechanism by which seals could impact crocodile numbers, thereby weakening the original claim that they aren\'t responsible..\n\nI think I\'ve got it now. Therefore, the option is ']] 27%|##6       | 161/600 [03:42<10:42,  1.46s/it]
