ign technology or patents..\n\nNow, looking at the options:.\n\nOption A: GM rice resists specific pests, but those pests aren't common in China. So, this might mean that the advantage isn't relevant here. But how does that support the expert's view? Maybe it's a weak point for GM rice, but I'm not sure it directly relates to state control..\n\nOption B: No GM rice surpasses current Chinese varieties in yield and quality. This suggests that GM rice isn't better, so maybe there's no need to adopt it. But again, does this directly support the idea that state control is threatened? It might make the case against GM rice stronger, but not necessarily about control..\n\nOption C: In the US, after introducing GM seeds, prices of seeds went up. This seems relevant because if seeds become more expensive, it could affect farmers' ability to plant without relying on buying new seeds each time. But does this tie into state control? Maybe, but I'm not sure if it's the strongest..\n\nOption D: If GM rice is grown, foreign patent holders will stop Chinese farmers from saving seeds, forcing them to buy new ones each season at high prices. This directly ties into the expert's concern about control. If farmers can't save seeds, they have to buy from the patent holders each time, which are foreign companies. This would mean China's control over its main food crop is weakened because they're dependent on foreign patents and companies for seeds. This seems to directly support the expert's view about losing control. Therefore, the option is "]]
