money → purchasing power → happiness. It\'s a linear cause-effect chain, not about multiple components leading to better effectiveness..\n\nOption B: A full-term baby loses an instinct after 2 months. If a 3-month-old baby still has the instinct, then the baby wasn\'t full-term. This is using a rule (instinct disappears by 2 months) and applying it to a case where the baby is 3 months old and still has the instinct, concluding the baby wasn\'t full-term. It\'s a form of modus tollens reasoning, which is different from the original argument\'s structure..\n\nOption C: Depending on size, a supermarket needs 1 or 3 security guards. If a supermarket uses 3 guards, it must be big. This is similar to the original argument in that it\'s using the number of guards (a component) to infer the size (a characteristic). The reasoning is that more guards imply a bigger store, just like more ingredients imply better efficacy. So, this seems similar because it\'s using the quantity of something (guards vs. ingredients) to make a conclusion about another characteristic (size vs. efficacy)..\n\nOption D: Electric current is like water flow. Thicker wires carry more electricity because larger pipes carry more water. This is an analogy using the principle of flow rate, where a larger diameter allows more flow. It\'s a direct proportionality argument, similar to the original\'s analogy about the chef and guests, but the structure is about proportionality rather than the number of components leading to effectiveness..\n\nWait, but the original argument\'s structure is about having more components (ingredients) leading to better effectiveness against resistance. So, in the context, it\'s about the number of ingredients making it harder for bacteria to resist. So, the reasoning is that more is better in terms of effectiveness because it\'s harder for the problem to overcome all components..\n\nLooking back at the options, option C is about the number of security guards (a component) indicating the size of the supermarket. So, more guards mean bigger store. That\'s similar in structure because it\'s using the quantity of a component to infer a characteristic. So, the reasoning is that the presence of more of something (guards) implies another characteristic (size). .\n\nOption D, on the other hand, is about the relationship between wire thickness and current, which is a direct proportionality, not about the number of components leading to effectiveness against a problem. Therefore, the option is ']] 30%|###       | 182/600 [06:31<06:43,  1.04it/s]
