messages:  [['Please act as a detective and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D".\n####\n# Context:\nIf logic comes from a priori, that is, logic transcends experience and exists before experience, then whether there is abstract logic that transcends concrete things if there is, how can we infer that many logicians often prove the transcendental nature of logic with propositions such as the sum of interior angles of triangles equal to 180 degrees-nature, especially as a symbolic system of mathematical logic.It seems that logical forms can have the self-consistency and eternal correctness of reasoning without relying on people\'s experience, and things in the objective world must be arranged through logical arrangement in order to make people have a clear and systematic understanding. If this is the case, we cannot help but ask how human beings, as empirical beings, know that there is no doubt that there is a logic that transcends experience, and that acknowledging the existence of transcendental logic is bound to fall into the quagmire of dogmatism or the rut of mysticism.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is the main content of this text?\n# Options:\nA) Questioning the existence of transcendental logic\nB) It shows that mysticism is undesirable\nC) Argumentation logic is a summary of experience\nD) Explaining the objective world is sometimes illogical\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', ".\nOkay, so I need to figure out the main content of the given text. Let me read through it carefully..\n\nThe text starts by talking about logic coming from a priori, meaning it exists before experience. It mentions that logic transcends experience and is eternal. The example given is the sum of interior angles of triangles being 180 degrees, which is a mathematical truth. The author seems to be pointing out that logical forms have self-consistency and correctness without relying on experience. .\n\nThen, the text questions how humans, who are empirical beings, can know that such transcendental logic exists. It suggests that acknowledging this might lead to dogmatism or mysticism. So, the author is raising doubts about the existence of logic that's beyond experience..\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) Questioning the existence of transcendental logic.\nB) It shows that mysticism is undesirable.\nC) Argumentation logic is a summary of experience.\nD) Explaining the objective world is sometimes illogical.\n\nThe text is mainly about whether transcendental logic exists, especially since humans are empirical. It's not directly about mysticism being bad (B), nor is it saying logic is a summary of experience (C). It's also not about the objective world being illogical (D). Therefore, the option is "]]
