m not sure if that's the case..\n\nAlternatively, option D says they spend 2 hours less per day on all household responsibilities, including dinner. So maybe they're more efficient in other areas, allowing them to spend the little time they do on dinner more effectively. But the context already mentions they spend 100 minutes less per week on dinner, so this option is adding that they're also spending less time on other tasks. Maybe this means they're more efficient overall, so the time they do spend on dinner is used more wisely, leading to similar quality..\n\nWait, but the problem is that they spend less time on dinner but the quality is the same. So the key is to find a reason why less time doesn't lead to worse quality. So perhaps they're using more efficient methods or have better skills. Alternatively, maybe they're not actually spending less time per meal but less time overall because they eat out more, but the context is about home dinners..\n\nWait, the context says that the dinners eaten at home by both groups don't differ. So the working adults are eating at home, but spending less time preparing those dinners. So why are their dinners as good? Maybe they're using more efficient methods, like quicker cooking or using pre-made ingredients. Alternatively, maybe they're eating simpler meals that take less time but are still nutritious..\n\nLooking at the options again, option B says they eat at home less often. So if they eat at home less, maybe the dinners they do prepare are more planned or they have more time per meal because they're preparing fewer meals. For example, if they eat at home three times a week instead of five, they might spend more time per meal, but overall less time. But the context says they spend 100 minutes less per week on dinner. So if they're preparing fewer dinners, the time per dinner could be the same or even more, but overall less time. But the context says they spend less time on dinner preparation, so maybe they're preparing the same number of dinners but in less time, which would require more efficiency..\n\nWait, but the context says they spend 100 minutes less each week. So if they're preparing the same number of dinners, they must be doing it more efficiently. But if they're preparing fewer dinners, then the time per dinner could be the same or even more, but overall less time. So if they eat at home less often, that could explain why their dinners are still good despite less total time spent. Because they're not preparing as many, so each dinner gets more attention or they use better methods..\n\nAlternatively, option D says they spend 2 hours less per day on all household responsibilities, including dinner. So maybe they're more efficient in other areas, allowing them to focus more on dinner when they do prepare it. But I'm not sure if that's the case..\n\nWait, the key is that the dinners are as good despite less time. So the explanation must be that the time saved is not on the dinner itself but elsewhere, or that the dinner preparation is more efficient..\n\nOption B says they eat at home less often. So if they eat at home less, maybe they're eating out more, but the context is about home dinners. So the dinners they do prepare at home are still good because they have more time per meal. For example, if they eat at home three times a week instead of five, they might spend more time on each meal, making them as good as the non-working adults who eat at home more often. So the total time spent on dinner would be less because they're preparing fewer meals, but each meal is as good because they spend more time per meal..\n\nAlternatively, maybe they're using more efficient methods, like quicker recipes, so they can prepare good meals in less time. But the options don't mention that..\n\nSo between B and D, I think B is more likely because it explains that they're eating at home less, so the time saved is because they're preparing fewer dinners, but the ones they do prepare are still