aybe this isn\'t directly relevant. I\'m not sure yet..\n\nOption C: "The spotted fruit bat dwells deep inside the caves where government workers, despite using sophisticated technology, have been unable to penetrate completely.".\n\nOh, this is interesting. If the government can\'t reach the bats because they\'re too deep in the caves, then cutting off the passageways might not work. Because if the workers can\'t get in, they can\'t block the passageways effectively. So the bats might still find a way out, or the plan might not be executed properly. This would mean the plan won\'t achieve its objective. So this seems like a strong candidate..\n\nOption D: "Many villagers who would have otherwise succumbed to a viral disease have been cured after ingesting a serum derived from a local flower whose only means of germinating is via the fur of the spotted fruit bat.".\n\nWait, this is about a serum that cures the disease, and the flower only germinates via the bat\'s fur. So if the government eliminates the bats, the flower can\'t germinate, so the serum can\'t be made. That means villagers who would have been cured won\'t be, leading to more deaths. So this shows that the plan could actually increase fatalities because it removes the cure. That\'s a good point against the plan..\n\nSo now I\'m torn between options C and D. Let me think again..\n\nOption C suggests that the plan might not be feasible because the workers can\'t reach the bats. So the plan might not even work as intended because they can\'t block the passageways. That would mean the bats can still emerge, so the virus isn\'t controlled..\n\nOption D suggests that eliminating the bats would stop the serum production, leading to more deaths. So even if the plan works and reduces the virus spread, it might cause another problem by removing the cure. But the question is about whether the plan will lead to its objective, which is reducing deaths. If the plan reduces virus spread but also removes the cure, the net effect on deaths isn\'t clear. It might reduce some deaths but increase others. However, the question is about the plan not leading to its objective, which is reducing deaths. So if the plan causes more deaths through the serum issue, then it\'s a problem..\n\nBut wait, the question is about the plan not leading to its objective. So if the plan fails to eliminate the bats (option C), then the virus isn\'t controlled, so deaths don\'t reduce. If the plan succeeds in eliminating the bats but causes more deaths because of the serum (option D), then the plan\'s objective isn\'t met because deaths might not go down, or might even increase..\n\nSo both C and D could indicate the plan won\'t work. But which one is stronger?.\n\nOption C is about the feasibility of the plan. If they can\'t penetrate the caves, the plan can\'t be executed properly, so the bats remain, and the virus continues. That directly shows the plan won\'t achieve its objective..\n\nOption D is about an unintended consequence. The plan might achieve its goal of reducing virus spread, but another factor (the serum) causes more deaths. So the net effect on village deaths isn\'t clear. It might not necessarily mean the plan fails to reduce deaths, but it complicates the outcome..\n\nThe question is asking which would best indicate that the plan won\'t lead to its objective. So if the plan can\'t be executed (C), then it\'s a sure failure. If the plan has a negative side effect (D), it\'s a problem but not necessarily a failure of the plan\'s main goal. Therefore, the option is ']] 49%|####9     | 294/600 [11:23<10:11,  2.00s/it]
