ince the green space is part of the community, it belongs to him because he\'s a member of the community. Therefore, he thinks he has the right to plant cucumbers there..\n\nHmm, so Xiao Li is making a logical leap here. He\'s taking the fact that the green space belongs to the community and assuming that because he\'s part of the community, it\'s his property. But that\'s not correct because community property doesn\'t mean individual ownership. The green space is shared, not his personal land. So his mistake is confusing collective ownership with individual rights..\n\nNow, looking at the options, I need to find the one that makes a similar logical error. Let\'s go through each option one by one..\n\nOption A: "Everyone should be responsible for his wrong behavior. Xiao Wang is not responsible for his behavior. All Xiao Wang\'s behavior is correct." Wait, this seems a bit confusing. It says that if someone isn\'t responsible, then all their behavior is correct. That doesn\'t quite match Xiao Li\'s mistake. Xiao Li\'s issue was about ownership, not responsibility..\n\nOption B: "All the orchids participating in the expo were ordered out. Li Yang bought a pot of flowers at a high price. It can be seen that Li Yang must buy orchids." So, the argument here is that because all orchids were ordered out, and Li Yang bought flowers, he must have bought orchids. That doesn\'t make sense because there could be other flowers besides orchids. This seems like a hasty generalization, assuming that the only flowers available were orchids. But Xiao Li\'s mistake was about ownership, not about generalization..\n\nOption C: "No one can read all Dumas\'s works in one day, and no one can read The Three Musketeers in one day. Therefore, The Three Musketeers is one of Dumas\'s works." This is interesting. The argument is that since both are impossible to read in a day, they must be related. But that\'s not necessarily true. Just because two things are hard to read in a day doesn\'t mean one is part of the other. This seems like a false cause or a non-sequitur. But again, it\'s not about ownership..\n\nOption D: "The army composed of all Mobi knights was invincible in Europe at that time, and the King Yiya was one of the Mobi knights, so the King Yiya was invincible in Europe at that time." Here, the argument is that because the entire army was invincible, each individual knight, including the King, must be invincible. But that\'s not necessarily true. The army\'s invincibility doesn\'t mean each member is invincible on their own. This is similar to Xiao Li\'s mistake because he\'s taking a collective property (the army\'s invincibility) and applying it to an individual (the King). Xiao Li did the same with the community\'s green space, assuming his individual right to use it because it\'s community property. Therefore, the option is ']] 54%|#####4    | 325/600 [12:02<08:08,  1.78s/it]
