ion is C, but that's actually supporting Li Ming. So perhaps the correct answer is D because it shows that even when the economy is bad, people (non-unemployed) can still spend more when prices drop, thus supporting Zhang Qiang's point that low prices can stimulate consumption..\n\nAlternatively, maybe Option A is about after the economy improves, which is not directly about the initial point. So perhaps the correct answer is D..\n\nWait, I'm getting a bit confused. Let me try to structure this..\n\nLi Ming's argument: Unemployed can't spend more because they have no money. So low prices won't help because they can't buy more than necessities..\n\nWe need an option that weakens this argument. So, an option that shows that even the unemployed can spend more when prices drop..\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) After economy improves, companies hire more, and re-employed buy postponed goods. This is about after the economy improves, not directly about whether low prices can stimulate consumption during the downturn..\n\nB) Consumers believe economy will improve, so they spend more. This is about confidence, not directly about the unemployed's ability to spend..\n\nC) Unemployed must buy necessities. This supports Li Ming's point..\n\nD) Non-unemployed cut back, but when prices drop, they have money to save for consumption. So this is about non-unemployed being able to spend more when prices drop, which would support Zhang Qiang's point that low prices can stimulate consumption, thereby weakening Li Ming's argument that the unemployed can't spend more..\n\nWait, but Li Ming's argument is specifically about the unemployed. So if the non-unemployed can spend more when prices drop, that would help the economy, but it doesn't directly address the unemployed's situation. So maybe the correct answer is D because it shows that even if some people are unemployed, others can spend more when prices drop, thus improving the economy..\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct answer is C