eir best remembered. So, does this mean that one can only correctly assess quality if it's from the past? James might be implying that, but Rachel doesn't address this. Rachel is more about the constraints, not the time factor. So, maybe they don't disagree on C..\n\nOption D: Contemporary art is of lower quality than earlier art. Rachel says that the freedom has caused a decline, so she believes contemporary art is worse. James argues that people think it's worse because they haven't forgotten the inferior works yet, but he's saying that this perception is mistaken. So, James doesn't think contemporary art is necessarily worse; it's just that we haven't had time to see which ones are great. Therefore, Rachel believes D is true, while James does not. So, they disagree on D..\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Rachel says that the freedom has caused a decline, so she's asserting that contemporary art is lower quality. James says that people think it's lower quality because they haven't forgotten the bad ones yet, but he's arguing against that perception, implying that contemporary art isn't actually worse. So yes, they disagree on whether contemporary art is of lower quality. Therefore, the option is "]] 59%|#####8    | 354/600 [12:27<06:17,  1.54s/it]
