ading by parents on mobile phones and computers has not been taken into account in children's books. So if parents are reading electronically, maybe that's not being counted towards the children's reading. But the context says that the amount of books read by parents and children increased. So if electronic reading is part of that, then it's already counted. Unless the children's reading isn't including electronic books, but the context doesn't specify. So I'm not sure if this is the best explanation..\n\nLet me think again. The key point is that while more families are reading together, the average number of books per child is down. So the increase in reading habits and book length might not translate to more books read per child. .\n\nOption C suggests that listening to books isn't counted. So if children are listening to more books (which counts as reading for the family), but they're not actually reading the books themselves, then the number of books each child reads individually might go down. That makes sense because the family's total reading time and content might be up, but the child's personal reading count is down because they're just listening instead of reading..\n\nOption A talks about schoolwork, but if the amount of books read by parents and children increased, that might mean they're making time despite schoolwork. So maybe A isn't the best fit. Therefore, the option is "]] 59%|#####9    | 356/600 [12:29<04:34,  1.13s/it]
