emical insecticides harm more insect species than B. t. toxins. Wait, but the argument says B. t. is better because it's less harmful. If chemical insecticides are worse, that actually supports the argument, not weakens it. So A might not be the answer..\n\nOption B: No single B. t. toxin works against all insects. Hmm, the context mentions that each toxin affects a limited range, so this is already implied. But does this weaken the argument? The argument is about B. t. being preferable because they don't affect non-pest insects. If they don't work against all pests, that might be a problem, but the argument is about their specificity, not their effectiveness across all pests. So maybe B is a point against, but I'm not sure yet..\n\nOption C: B. t. doesn't harm weeds that damage crops. Wait, the context is about insects, not weeds. So this seems irrelevant. The argument is about insects, so C probably doesn't weaken the argument..\n\nOption D: Insects develop resistance more quickly to B. t. toxins than to chemicals. Oh, that's a big issue. If pests become resistant faster to B. t., then the effectiveness of B. t. would decrease over time. This would make chemical insecticides more reliable in the long run, which weakens the argument that B. t. is preferable. So D seems like a strong candidate..\n\nWait, let me think again. The argument's main point is that B. t. is better because it's specific and doesn't harm other insects. If D is true, it introduces a problem with B. t.'s effectiveness over time, which is a significant weakness. So D would make B. t. less preferable because they might not work as well in the future. Therefore, the option is "]]
