tor's actions. This would actually strengthen the solicitor's argument, not weaken it. So C is probably not the answer..\n\nOption D: Loux's grandson had repeatedly expressed his desire to own a farm. This is interesting. If the grandson really wanted the farm, then Loux might have wanted to give it to him despite not expressing it explicitly. The solicitor's argument is that since she didn't express a desire, she wouldn't mind selling it. But if the grandson wanted it, maybe Loux would have wanted him to have it, even if she didn't say so. So this weakens the solicitor's argument because it suggests that there was an implicit desire to keep the farm in the family, which the solicitor didn't consider..\n\nWait, so between B and D. Let me think again. The solicitor's argument is based on Loux's lack of expressed desire. If the debts couldn't be cleared without selling the farm (B), then Zembaty had no choice, so the argument about Loux's approval is irrelevant because it was necessary. That would weaken the argument because it shows that the sale wasn't just about her preference but was a necessity. On the other hand, D suggests that there was an implicit desire because the grandson wanted it, which the solicitor didn't consider, thus weakening the argument..\n\nI think D is a stronger weakening because it introduces a reason why Loux might have wanted the farm to go to her grandson despite not expressing it. B is more about the necessity of selling, which might not directly weaken the argument about her approval but rather explains why it had to be done regardless of her wishes..\n\nWait, but the question is about weakening the solicitor's argument. The solicitor's argument is that Loux wouldn't mind selling because she didn't express a desire. If D is true, then even though she didn't express it, she might have wanted the grandson to have it because he wanted it. So the solicitor's assumption that her silence means she doesn't care is wrong because there's an implicit desire. Therefore, the option is "]]
