he same amount, the traffic deaths have gone down. The question is asking which option, if true, would help resolve this discrepancy..\n\nLet me break this down. The key points are:.\n\n1. Legal drinking age increased from 18 to 21 in 1990..\n2. Goal: reduce alcohol consumption → reduce traffic deaths among under 21..\n3. Result: Traffic deaths under 21 decreased, but alcohol consumption in that group didn't change..\n\nSo the problem is that even though they're drinking the same amount, fewer are dying in traffic accidents. Why is that?.\n\nI need to find an option that explains why the number of deaths went down without a decrease in alcohol consumption. Let's look at the options one by one..\n\nOption A: Alcohol-related traffic deaths among people over 21 have increased slightly since 1990..\n\nHmm, so if more older people are dying, does that explain why younger people's deaths went down? Maybe, but it doesn't directly address why the same amount of drinking isn't leading to more deaths. It's more about shifting the problem to another group, but the question is about resolving the discrepancy in the under-21 group. So maybe not the best option..\n\nOption B: For the population as a whole, annual alcohol consumption is no lower now than it was in 1990..\n\nWait, so overall, people are drinking the same or more. But the under-21 group is drinking the same as before. So this doesn't directly explain why their traffic deaths went down. It just says that overall consumption hasn't decreased, which might support the idea that the law didn't reduce consumption, but it doesn't explain the drop in deaths. So probably not the answer..\n\nOption C: The proportion of people under 21 who own a car is higher now than it was in 1990..\n\nWait, that seems counterintuitive. If more under-21s own cars, wouldn't that mean more potential drivers, possibly leading to more accidents? But the traffic deaths went down. So maybe this isn't the right direction. Or perhaps it's the opposite: if fewer own cars, but that's not what the option says. So maybe this isn't the answer either..\n\nOption D: Alcohol consumption away from home, for example in bars and restaurants, is much lower among people under 21 than it was in 1990..\n\nOkay, so they're drinking the same amount overall, but less in public places like bars and restaurants. Why would that matter? Well, if they're drinking at home, maybe they're not driving after drinking. Because if you're at a bar, you might drive home, but if you're drinking at home, you're not. So even though they're drinking the same amount, they're not driving after drinking as much, hence fewer traffic deaths..\n\nThat makes sense. So the key is that the location of drinking changed. They're not drinking in places where they might drive afterward, so even though they're consuming the same amount, the risk of driving under the influence is reduced..\n\nSo putting it all together, the discrepancy is that despite the same alcohol consumption, traffic deaths decreased. The reason is that the drinking is happening in places where they're not driving, so fewer accidents. Therefore, the option is "]] 71%|#######   | 424/600 [19:11<01:10,  2.50it/s] 71%|#######   | 425/600 [19:11<01:07,  2.61it/s]
