ou touch something hot, your body automatically pulls away to avoid pain. This is more of a reflex (like a withdrawal reflex) rather than a learned avoidance. So, this might not be avoidance conditioning..\n\nOption C: Leave temporarily when you feel noisy in the house. If someone leaves a noisy environment to avoid the discomfort, that's an example of avoidance conditioning. They're escaping an aversive stimulus (noise) and are likely to do this again when the environment becomes noisy..\n\nOption D: Afraid to meet strangers and dare not go to the streets. This seems like a learned behavior where the person avoids situations that cause fear or anxiety. They might have had a bad experience with strangers before, so they avoid going out. This is definitely avoidance conditioning because they're escaping an aversive situation and are more likely to avoid it in the future..\n\nSo, putting it all together, the options that are examples of avoidance conditioning are A, C, and D. Therefore, the option is "]]
