w harm because people are already ingesting natural toxins without issues..\n\nThe options are A, B, C, D. I need to figure out which one doesn't weaken the argument..\n\nLet me break down the argument. The main point is that since people have been consuming natural drugs in plants without harm, synthetic pesticides, which are also chemicals, don't pose a significant new risk. So, the argument relies on the idea that natural and synthetic pesticides are similar in terms of their effects on humans..\n\nNow, each option is a potential statement that could either support or undermine this argument. I need to find the one that doesn't undermine it..\n\nOption A: The concentration of natural drugs in plants is much lower than that of synthetic pesticides. Hmm, if synthetic pesticides are more concentrated, that could mean they're more potent or harmful, which would undermine the argument because the argument assumes that because natural ones are low in concentration and safe, synthetic ones are too. So A could be a problem for the argument..\n\nOption B: Humans have been ingesting these natural drugs for thousands of years and have adapted. This actually supports the argument because it explains why people aren't harmed—they've had time to adapt. So B doesn't undermine the argument; it strengthens it..\n\nOption C: Synthetic pesticides have simpler chemical structures than natural drugs. The context mentions that natural drugs are complex. If synthetic ones are simpler, maybe they're more easily broken down or less toxic. But wait, the argument is that because people are used to complex natural toxins, synthetic ones aren't a big deal. If synthetic are simpler, maybe they're less harmful, which would support the argument. Alternatively, maybe simpler structures could mean they're more effective or more toxic. I'm not sure yet. I'll come back to this..\n\nOption D: Natural drugs target specific organisms, while synthetic pesticides harm many. This could be a problem becau