al driving force of research..\nC) Appreciation is an important factor in success..\nD) Finding problems is the premise of continuous progress..\n\nSo, the paragraph emphasizes that Wittgenstein\'s questioning and endless questions were a key factor in his success. Moore pointed out that he always had questions, which suggests that his curiosity and the act of questioning were important. Wittgenstein later said that Russell fell behind because he had no problem, meaning he didn\'t have the same drive to question or find issues..\n\nOption A talks about doubting and questioning being the secret of success. That seems relevant because Wittgenstein\'s questioning was highlighted. Option B is about curiosity as a driving force, which also fits because curiosity leads to asking questions. Option D mentions finding problems as a premise for progress, which aligns with Wittgenstein\'s approach of always having questions, implying he was finding problems to solve..\n\nWait, but the paragraph specifically mentions that Wittgenstein had endless questions, which suggests he was always curious and questioning. So both A and B seem close. However, the fact that Wittgenstein said Russell had no problem might point more towards the idea that finding problems is essential. Because if you don\'t have problems (i.e., you don\'t identify issues to solve), you can\'t progress. So D might be the better choice..\n\nAlternatively, A and B are about the act of questioning and curiosity, which are related but perhaps more about the mindset. D is about the necessity of identifying problems for progress..\n\nI think the main point is that without problems, you can\'t make progress, as Wittgenstein said about Russell. So the paragraph is illustrating that finding problems is the premise of continuous progress. Therefore, the option is ']]
