ed on these trial results..\n\nHmm, so the company's plan is to have everyone work from home because the trial with volunteers was successful. But the question is about finding a reason why this plan shouldn't be implemented despite the trial's positive results. So I need to find an option that points out a flaw in the reasoning or a limitation of the trial..\n\nLet's look at the options one by one..\n\nOption A: The employees who agreed to participate were among the most self-motivated and independent. Wait, that makes sense because if only the most motivated employees volunteered, their productivity might not be representative of the entire company. People who are less motivated might struggle working from home, leading to lower productivity. So this could be a good argument against the plan because the trial didn't test a representative sample..\n\nOption B: The volunteers could communicate as needed. That seems positive because communication is important. If they could communicate well, it supports the plan. So this wouldn't argue against it; in fact, it might support it..\n\nOption C: The savings alone justify the plan. This is about the cost savings being enough, regardless of productivity. But the trial showed productivity was the same or higher, so this actually supports the plan. It doesn't argue against it..\n\nOption D: Minor changes in office organization could yield similar productivity increases. So if the company can get similar results by just changing how they organize the office, then implementing the work-from-home plan might not be necessary. That could argue against the plan because there's a simpler solution. But I'm not sure if this is the strongest argument..\n\nNow, comparing A and D. Option A points out that the volunteers were a specific group, so the results might not apply to everyone. This is a common issue in trials where the sample isn't representative. If only the top employees were tested, the plan might fail when applied to the general workforce. On the other hand, Option D suggests an alternative solution, but it's more about whether the plan is the best option rather than whether the trial results are reliable. Therefore, the option is "]] 89%|########8 | 533/600 [21:31<00:56,  1.19it/s]
