uld also flow to the northwest." But from the context, we know that the Han River actually flows southeast, not northwest. So, Li Bai is saying that the Han River should flow northwest, but in reality, it doesn\'t..\n\nSo, Li Bai is making a statement that if fame and wealth last a long time, then the Han River should flow northwest. But since the Han River doesn\'t flow northwest, that implies that fame and wealth cannot last a long time. Because if they could, the Han River would be flowing northwest, which it isn\'t..\n\nSo, the structure is like a conditional statement. Let me represent it logically. Let\'s say:.\n\nIf Fame and wealth exist for a long time (F), then the Han River flows northwest (H)..\n\nIn logical terms, that\'s F → H..\n\nBut we know that H is false because the Han River flows southeast. So, if F → H is true, and H is false, then F must be false. Because in logic, if the consequent is false, the antecedent must be false for the implication to hold..\n\nSo, F is false, meaning Fame and wealth do not exist for a long time. Therefore, the correct interpretation is that fame and wealth cannot always be there..\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) Fame and wealth can exist for a long time, but the Han River should not flow to the northwest. Wait, this seems to contradict because if fame and wealth can exist, then according to Li Bai, the Han River should flow northwest. But the Han River doesn\'t, so this might not be correct..\n\nB) If fame and wealth do not exist for a long time, the Han River should not flow to the northwest. Hmm, this is a bit tricky. The original statement is F → H. The contrapositive would be ¬H → ¬F. So, if the Han River doesn\'t flow northwest (¬H), then fame and wealth don\'t exist for a long time (¬F). But option B is saying ¬F → ¬H, which is the inverse of the original implication. The inverse isn\'t necessarily true just because the original is. So B might not be correct..\n\nC) Fame and wealth cannot always be there. This